  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of the 14-year-old girl, including exertional leg pain, obesity, broad neck, low-set ears, and delayed lower leg pulses, is suggestive of Turner syndrome. Turner syndrome is characterized by various physical anomalies and is commonly associated with ovarian dysgenesis, which is a lack of normal ovarian development. The presence of a broad neck and excess skin folds further supports this diagnosis. While absent uterus is also a potential finding in Turner syndrome, ovarian dysgenesis is more directly linked to the condition. Mitral valve prolapse is a known association with Turner syndrome but is less definitive than ovarian dysgenesis. Triphalangeal thumb and horseshoe adrenal gland are not typical findings in Turner syndrome. Therefore, the most likely additional finding in this patient is ovarian dysgenesis."
